          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "You should seek care. A high TSH level with normal free T4 indicates subclinical hypothyroidism, which requires monitoring and may need treatment depending on your specific situation.\n\nThe guidance around treating subclinical hypothyroidism varies. If your TSH is consistently above around 10 mIU/L, treatment is generally recommended. If it is below 10 mIU/L, some guidelines suggest monitoring without immediate treatment unless symptoms or risk factors are present (condition-hypothyroidism).\n\nFor monitoring, rechecking TSH levels is typically recommended every 6 to 12 months for those diagnosed with subclinical hypothyroidism. If there are changes in symptoms or medication adjustments, more frequent checks may be warranted (Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance). However, some guidelines advise rechecking TSH levels about 6 to 8 weeks after initiating treatment or modifying any treatment (guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring).\n\nIt is essential to discuss your results and management plan with your healthcare provider to determine the best approach for your individual situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
